Gozar is a Firefox VPN client for Android, Windows, macOS, and Linux.

Android uses its existing Kotlin application and its shared core. Desktop has a native Flutter
Material 3 interface and a Go core. It does not use Kotlin, a JVM, a browser-based interface, or
an HTTP API for the interface. The Flutter process communicates with the Go process through a
private standard-input/output pipe. The interface supports Persian, English, and the system
language, and keeps the selected language between launches. Android also offers the language
picker on the sign-in screen and in Settings. Desktop registration and email verification
use native forms; supported Mozilla security checks and image challenges appear in the app. The
account password is not saved. Separate Firefox sessions can be added and switched inside the app;
switching, adding an account while connected, or signing out disconnects the VPN first. Signing out
removes the active session while leaving other saved sessions available from the sign-in screen.
Android encrypts saved session tokens using Android Keystore where available, with an app-private
software-key fallback. Desktop uses Windows user-bound protection or AES-GCM with a private local
key on Unix; its older session-token helper can fall back to plaintext if protection fails. WARP
device credentials use separate encrypted storage and fail closed rather than falling back to
plaintext. All credentials remain sensitive, especially to other processes running as the same user.

Android adds the GlobalSign E46 and R46 roots only for fastly-masque.net and its subdomains,
while continuing to verify certificate chains and server hostnames. Its HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1
CONNECT paths and packet handling have loopback and in-memory tests. The user reports that a friend
successfully connected on the previously affected physical Android 13 phone after the certificate
fix. Device certificate stores and server chains vary, so this is not a guarantee for all devices.
The app does not keep its previous in-memory diagnostics or crash file.

Android exposes the system's Always-on VPN and Block connections without VPN settings rather
than pretending an app-only switch can provide system lockdown. Status is available only while
an established VPN service can query Android 10 or later. Direct TCP rules are disabled during
lockdown, whenever status cannot be checked, and on Android 9 or earlier. LAN bypass is TCP-only;
local-network discovery and other UDP traffic do not use that TCP bypass. Per-app selection, experimental
root routing and system-level exceptions have separate limitations. Domain-based direct rules
use DNS-to-IP associations, so shared IP addresses can make the match ambiguous.

Android offers opt-in WARP CONNECT-IP for captured non-DNS IPv4 UDP. Enabling it requires accepting
Cloudflare's terms and automatically scans endpoints after the Mozilla VPN connects. Captured IPv6
is dropped; excluded apps and experimental root routing have separate limitations. The setting is
experimental and does not guarantee UDP on every network or device.

Android DNS can use the built-in encrypted providers or a custom HTTPS DNS endpoint. The optional
ad/tracker protection selects AdGuard DNS without falling back to an unfiltered resolver. It
blocks many domains, not all ads: first-party ads, applications using their own encrypted DNS,
and traffic outside this VPN are not reliably filtered. DNS requests handled by Gozar travel
through the authenticated proxy. The app shows an ongoing notification and an estimated session
traffic chart. Promoted notifications and Xiaomi Focus/Super Island depend on system support,
settings and, for Xiaomi, authorization. The new security and notification behavior has not been
verified on a physical Android or Xiaomi device.

On Android, the sign-in email button opens Android's system account chooser. It accepts only
selected account names that are valid email addresses; manual entry remains available. It does
not perform Google Sign-In or use Gozar's saved sessions as system accounts. On Linux the equivalent
button reads email-like identities from GNOME Online Accounts on explicit request, when that
service is available. Windows and macOS do not provide a verified universal, provider-independent
system email picker in this build, so manual entry is used there. The saved Gozar account switcher
is a separate feature on all platforms.

For a separate local Android test installation, build with `-PgozarDiagnostic`. This uses the
`com.gozar.diagnostic` package and a separate app data directory. Without the official signing
credentials, a local build is not an update to the official Gozar installation.

Desktop builds include a Windows EXE installer and Linux DEB alongside portable archives; macOS
uses .tar.xz archives. The same Linux build is also published as a Flatpak, a Snap, an AppImage,
Fedora and RHEL RPMs and an Arch package, and the AUR recipe is in `packaging/aur`. OpenWrt,
Raspberry Pi and FreeBSD receive the headless core with an init script instead of the Flutter
window, because those systems have no desktop session. They are not MSI or DMG installers. The Windows build contains Wintun. The desktop tunnel configures a
TUN interface and uses a userspace network stack; it needs administrator privileges. On Windows,
the application requests elevation when it starts. On Linux and macOS, launch the application
with appropriate administrator privileges to use the tunnel. The new desktop app does not set
or automatically clear a system proxy. If an older version left a proxy behind, review the system
proxy settings manually rather than assuming that a proxy belongs to Gozar. Abrupt termination can
leave routes or DNS modified, especially on Linux and macOS. Windows installers are not
Authenticode-signed, and macOS builds are not notarized. Desktop DNS queries
use DNS over HTTPS through the authenticated Firefox proxy. Optional non-DNS IPv4 UDP uses
Cloudflare WARP CONNECT-IP inside the authenticated Mozilla tunnel after explicit terms consent;
when unavailable it is refused instead of silently falling back to direct UDP. Windows route
commands do not open console windows.

The compact desktop dashboard uses a frameless window, a draggable header, and a real connect
control. Relay location, exit address, traffic and usage are shown only when available. The UI's
firewall control is unavailable; the backend can install operating-system firewall rules during a
connection, with explicit LAN and relay exceptions and platform-specific failure modes. On ordinary
window close, the application requests tunnel shutdown before exiting; forced termination is different.

The desktop tunnel is experimental. Real Linux, Windows and macOS tunnel interfaces were tested
with local simulated upstreams; the Linux code has also been tested with a local TLS-verified
HTTP/2 CONNECT relay. A separate authorized live script authenticated a real Firefox account,
refreshed its token and reached a Mozilla relay. CI-built Kotlin and Go WARP cores also passed UDP
round trips through a restricted bridge into that relay. Those isolated results do not prove the
complete Android or desktop app on a physical device. See docs/real-account-validation.md.
Mozilla may present an unsupported security challenge or refuse a request. Desktop IPv6 and some
existing LAN or relay routes may bypass the tunnel, including UDP. With WARP enabled, selective
direct desktop split routes are paused, but this is not device-wide leak protection. WARP uses
HTTP/2 over TCP and supports bounded IPv4 datagrams only. Do not rely on this build for privacy
or as a complete device-wide VPN. The desktop interface provides language, appearance, account
switching, recent-server preference, a manual release check, donation, and an ordered SOCKS5/HTTP
proxy-chain editor. Proxy chains are used by the Go core when connecting; proxy authentication is
not supported. Desktop per-application and per-domain routing, a dedicated LAN-bypass control,
and the route map are not implemented. Existing OS LAN routes may still bypass the tunnel.

Planned desktop work, subject to feasibility and testing, includes application-based routing
on Windows and Linux first; domain rules, an explicit LAN-bypass option and a route view;
provider-supported system-email selection; investigation of OS-backed token storage; and
better recovery and connectivity diagnostics. These are not implemented today. macOS per-app
routing and any additional IPv6 or UDP coverage require separate investigation, and arbitrary
UDP or leak-free VPN coverage is not promised. See the release notes for details.

Linux build, from the repository root:

    cd desktop_flutter
    flutter pub get
    flutter build linux --release
    cd ../go-desktop
    CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags='-s -w' -o ../desktop_flutter/build/linux/x64/release/bundle/gozar-core ./cmd/gozar

The desktop build needs Flutter 3.24.5, Go 1.24, and the platform's native build dependencies.
On Linux, the built app requires GTK 3 and a graphical desktop. A Flutter bundle contains
multiple files: keep the Go core, the executable, and the bundled data and libraries together.
macOS downloads use .tar.xz; extract them in Terminal with `tar -xJf ARCHIVE.tar.xz`.

